$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1\\times 9}{3\\times 9} & =\\frac{9}{27}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| $4$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $8$ | $24$ |\n| $9$ | $27$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455446 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455446 #edb/ref 1069 "q-59921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455446 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455446 #edb/ref 1071 "From top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table below?\n\n| $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $?$ | $24$ |\n| $30$ | $?$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455446 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40bbff9d-d33b-5344-bec9-19d7e575474d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455446 #edb/ref 1074 "We start with the ratio $5:6$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry ($5$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry ($6$).\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a denominator of $24,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\times 4}{6\\times 4}=\\frac{20}{24}\n$$\n\n- To create a numerator of $30,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $6:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\times 6}{6\\times 6}=\\frac{30}{36}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $20$ | $24$ |\n| $30$ | $36$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $20$ and $36.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455465 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455465 #edb/ref 1069 "q-59926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455465 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455465 #edb/ref 1071 "From top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table below?\n\n| $2$ | $?$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $?$ | $16$ |\n| $6$ | $24$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455465 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6997b2fa-6238-5241-b0d8-8e5218a5b865" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455465 #edb/ref 1074 "We start with the ratio $6:24$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{24}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry ($6$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry ($24$).\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a numerator of $2,$ we divide the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{6÷3}{24÷3}=\\frac{2}{8}\n$$\n\n- To create a denominator of $16,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of the previous fraction by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\times 2}{8\\times 2}=\\frac{4}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| $2$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $16$ |\n| $6$ | $24$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $8$ and $4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455467 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455467 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455467 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455467 #edb/ref 1071 "In one hour, a crew of $4$ painters can cover $28$ square meters of a wall's surface in a newly constructed building. A construction manager has created a ratio table to determine how many square meters of the wall's surface can be covered in one hour by crews of $6$ and $9$ painters.\n\n| Painters | Square meters |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $28$ |\n| $6$ | $?$ |\n| $9$ | $?$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455467 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d7ddf31-a7e6-5f18-99b9-b50c085a5cc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455467 #edb/ref 1074 "We start with the ratio $4:28$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{28}\n$$\n\nNow we divide the numerator and the denominator by $4$ to find an equivalent ratio:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4÷4}{28÷4}=\\frac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nWe can generate ratios that are equivalent to $1:7$ by finding more equivalent fractions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1\\times 6}{7\\times 6} & =\\frac{6}{42} \\\\ \\frac{1\\times 9}{7\\times 9} & =\\frac{9}{63}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| Painters | Square meters |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $28$ |\n| $6$ | $42$ |\n| $9$ | $63$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $42$ and $63.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455472 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455472 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455472 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455472 #edb/ref 1069 "q-186855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455472 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455472 #edb/ref 1071 "Complete the ratio table below.\n\n| $4$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $16$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $20$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $28$ | {{field-3}} |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455472 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7be9417a-a8b2-55e8-a3d3-285cb2bc9e4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455472 #edb/ref 1074 "We start with the ratio $4:1$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry ($4$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry ($1$).\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a numerator of $16,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\times 4}{1\\times 4}=\\frac{16}{4}\n$$\n\n- To create a numerator of $20,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\times 5}{1\\times 5}=\\frac{20}{5}\n$$\n\n- To create a numerator of $28,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $7:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\times 7}{1\\times 7}=\\frac{28}{7}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| $4$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $16$ | $4$ |\n| $20$ | $5$ |\n| $28$ | $7$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455473 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455473 #edb/ref 1069 "q-59908" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455473 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455473 #edb/ref 1071 "| $4$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $12$ | $?$ |\n| $20$ | $40$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWhat number is missing from the ratio table?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455473 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8252affb-ce2c-5680-9903-2378ac435a36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455473 #edb/ref 1074 "We start with the ratio $4:8$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{8}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry ($4$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry ($8$).\n\nWe can make a numerator of $12$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4\\times 3}{8\\times 3} & =\\frac{12}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our ratio table:\n\n| $4$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $12$ | $24$ |\n| $20$ | $40$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $24.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455501 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455501 #edb/ref 1069 "q-59919" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455501 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455501 #edb/ref 1071 "From top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table below?\n\n| $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $12$ | $?$ |\n| $?$ | $30$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455501 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dbd602d5-dd56-5f28-af09-18b7c946837e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455501 #edb/ref 1074 "We start with the ratio $3:5$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry ($3$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry ($5$).\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a numerator of $12,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 4}{5\\times 4}=\\frac{12}{20}\n$$\n\n- To create a denominator of $30,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $6:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 6}{5\\times 6}=\\frac{18}{30}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $12$ | $20$ |\n| $18$ | $30$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $20$ and $18.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455502 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455502 #edb/ref 1069 "q-92829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455502 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455502 #edb/ref 1071 "Liam wants to paint a fence. He knows he needs $2$ liters of paint for $32$ square meters of fence. He creates a ratio table to determine the ratio of the volume of paint to the fenced area for different quantities.\n\n| Fence area (square meters) | Paint (liters) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $32$ | $2$ |\n| $80$ | $?$ |\n| $160$ | $10$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWhat volume of paint does Liam need to paint $80$ square meters of the fence?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455502 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dbfbb926-45f3-5b9a-aee3-a655234da83d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455502 #edb/ref 1074 "We start with the ratio $32:2$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{32}{2}\n$$\n\nNow we divide the numerator and the denominator by $2$ to find an equivalent ratio:\n\n$$\n\\frac{32÷2}{2÷2}=\\frac{16}{1}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the area of the fence in ${\\text{m}}^{2}$ ($16$), and the denominator represents the volume of paint in liters ($1$).\n\nWe can make a numerator of $80$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16\\times 5}{1\\times 5} & =\\frac{80}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our ratio table:\n\n| Fence area (square meters) | Paint (liters) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $32$ | $2$ |\n| $80$ | $5$ |\n| $160$ | $10$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nSo, Liam needs $5$ liters of paint for $80$ square meters of the fence." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
